<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ArchUnitNET over NetArchTest.Rules: NetArchTest's last release is 1.3.2 (May 2021) and it
    pins Mono.Cecil 0.11.3, which predates every .NET release this solution targets. ArchUnitNET
    ships releases against current runtimes (0.13.4, August 2026) on Mono.Cecil 0.11.6, so the
    IL reader that decides whether these rules pass is one that has actually seen net10.0
    metadata. The .xUnit package is the xunit v2 assertion bridge, matching the xunit 2.9.3 used
    by the other two test projects.

    ArchUnitNET also refuses to pass a rule that matched nothing ("the rule requires positive
    evaluation, not just absence of violations"), which is the failure mode that matters most
    here: an architecture test whose predicate has drifted out of date reports a green tick over
    a boundary it is no longer looking at. NetArchTest passes such a rule silently.

    Mono.Cecil is referenced explicitly rather than leaned on transitively: ClockRules and
    PersistenceBoundaryRules read method bodies directly, which is a first-class dependency of
    this project and should be visible in the manifest rather than inherited by accident.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Mono.Cecil" Version="0.11.6" />
    <PackageReference Include="TngTech.ArchUnitNET" Version="0.13.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnit" Version="0.13.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    All three production projects are referenced so the rules can be stated as facts about real
    types rather than about strings. That is deliberate and safe: this project is a leaf that
    nothing else references, so referencing the Api here cannot create a cycle or smuggle a
    dependency into the layers under test.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\VelaCommerce.Api\VelaCommerce.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\VelaCommerce.Domain\VelaCommerce.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\VelaCommerce.Infrastructure\VelaCommerce.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
